              #region | "A-D Group Policy Formulas-Phase 01.ps1" |
              Group_Policy_Formulas_Phase_01 = $( # GPF = Group Policy Formula |
                @(
                  #region | security08_ | Mitigations for security vulnerabilities |
                  <# IMPORTANT BREAKING CHANGE |
                    CVE-2022-38042 required a new mandatory security check in AD that results in a breaking change. 
                    start msedge.exe 'https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/topic/kb5020276-netjoin-domain-join-hardening-changes-2b65a0f3-1f4c-42ef-ac0f-1caaf421baf8'
                    Domain-join operations are now _BLOCKED_ when reusing an existing AD Computer object _IF_ a certain set of conditions are in place. 

                    Going forward the routinely performed sequence of... 
                    domain-unjoin -> reboot -> domain-rejoin 
                    ...will fail IF a certain set of frequently established conditions are in place. 

                    Boolean representation for these blocking conditions: 
                    (
                      CONDITION_1: The AD User account attempting the domain-join is the CREATOR of the existing AD Computer object being reused.
                    )
                    -OR-
                    (
                      CONDITION_2: AD Computer object being reused was CREATED by a member of Domain Admins, regardless of 
                      - which AD User account is attempting the domain-join...
                      - whether the current Owner of the AD Computer object is a member of Domain Admins...
                      - whether the AD User account that created the AD Computer object currently holds membership in Domain Admins! 
                    )
                    -OR-
                    (
                      CONDITION_3: The OWNER of the AD Computer object that is being reused is referenced within the policy setting
                      "Domain controller: Allow computer account re-use during domain join" of a GPO scoped to OU=Domain Controllers

                      NOTE: Default status of this policy setting is Not Defined. 
                      EXPLANATION: 
                      - This security setting determines whether the domain controller will allow a client identity to attempt to re-use 
                      an existing computer account OWNED by a different identity during domain join.
                      - By default, the following OWNERS are trusted: Administrators and THE USER PERFORMING THE DOMAIN JOIN. 
                      - When this policy is configured with a list of trusted users or groups, the domain controller will allow a client 
                      to re-use a computer account that is OWNED by a member of the specified group (or specified identity) during domain join.
                      - This policy should be applied to all domain controllers in a domain by enabling the policy on the Domain Controllers OU.
                      ASIDE: 
                      - Realize that a policy setting whose status is 'Not Defined' doesn't always mean that the policy setting is inert and does nothing! 
                      - When a policy setting's status is 'Not Defined' that policy setting could still have an influence on something, somewhere, and in some way. 
                    )

                    What have we learned? 
                    TAKEAWAY_1: One AD User account should CREATE the AD Computer object and an entirely separate AD User account should perform 
                    the DOMAIN-JOIN on the instance of Windows/Linux. 
                    TAKEAWAY_2: Do not let a member of Domain Admins create AD Computer objects! 
                    TAKEAWAY_3: Consider which account is the Owner of an AD Computer object! Still need to confirm, but it appears that the 
                    default Owner of an AD Computer object is also the AD User account who CREATED that very same AD Computer object. 
                    TAKEAWAY_4: Yet another manual item to remember when pushing code beyond Dev. MANUAL_PRODUCTION_TASK

                    Scroll down to 'August 13, 2024' for instructions
                    CompConf > Pol > Win > Sec > Local Policies > Security Options > 
                    Edit policy setting "Domain controller: Allow computer account re-use during domain join" > 
                    Edit Security... > 
                    Add Groups of trusted computer account creators and owners to the Allow permission

                    KB5020276Ã¢â‚¬â€Netjoin: Domain join hardening changes
                    Elevation of Privilege Vulnerability from CVE-2022-38042
                    start msedge.exe 'https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2022-38042'
                  #>
                  @{ # security08_Harden Domain-Join Operations_CVE-2022-38042 |
                    Reference = $(
                      $TargetGpoName = 'security08_Harden Domain-Join Operations_CVE-2022-38042'
